\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pathfinder-note}

\title{Action-Dependent Frames for Tendon-Driven Manipulation}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{The pair}

Q presents Aero Hand Open, an underactuated tendon-driven hand whose
simulation and actuation interface support learned in-hand cube rotation
using seven motor encoders.
P presents DynaMAC, which adapts multi-stream imitation policies to
dynamic and bimanual manipulation by removing kinematically linked
reference frames and supplementing them with virtual end-effector
frames.
\ledger{1,7}

\section{The connexion pursued}

The initial question was whether P's whole-frame removal discards useful
within-grasp motion constraints when applied to Q's dexterous hand.
An object can respond to the hand's actions while moving relative to its
palm: action dependence does not imply rigid attachment.
The proposed distinction was between common wrist--object motion and
controlled finger--object motion.
\ledger{1,3,7}

The first investigation established local examples and a sensing
ambiguity, but its proposed factor construction was already covered by
task-space products of experts.
Subsequent investigations therefore examined feedback from retained
action-dependent frames, first with freely specified precisions, then
with precisions constrained by demonstrations, and finally with
articulated and compliant responses.
The retained results and their qualifications appear below.
\ledger{4,8,11,13,21,23,30,32,38}

The latest verifier review is entry 40.
It accepted the restricted calculations but judged the material thin:
the general mechanism overlaps with performative prediction, the
previous experiments did not require retaining the frame, Q was
unnecessary, and no effect on P had been demonstrated.
It requested a bounded investigation of correlated experts and an
informative reference whose deletion would have a measurable cost.
The appended investigation answers that request.
\ledger{40,41,48}

The new result separates covariance calibration from motion generation.
With independent frame noise, population joint conditioning removes
the growing modes of the independently fitted surrogate, but can
exactly reproduce the current state and generate no motion.
With an informative external reference, conditioning can improve on
deletion or itself become unstable.
Static-reference trajectories permit an elementary response estimate
in the constructed experiment, under assumptions not supplied by Q.
These are established statistical and identification techniques, not a
new robotics method.
The current account is \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md},
lines 3--19 and 279--285.
\ledger{42,43,44,45,47,48}

The recommendation is to pause the original Q/P publication and
two-hand hardware integration.
This is a recommendation recorded at the end of the peer phase, not a
claim that a publication-level result has been achieved.
The narrower analysis may support a technical explanation, but its
novelty and practical significance remain unresolved.
\ledger{48,49}

\section{Retained findings from the first round}

\subsection{Precision does not establish rigid attachment}

P thresholds phase-conditioned stream covariance.
A changing mean object-to-palm pose therefore need not produce large
covariance: repeatable in-hand rotation can remain precise at every
phase.
This corrects the initial interpretation.
P also allows temporal filtering of brief false positives, so its
detector must be evaluated rather than presumed to fail.
Its per-skill algorithm does not fully specify an online contact
estimator.
\ledger{1,2,7,11}

In normalized coordinates, with principal standard deviations
\(\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_6\), P's statistic is
\[
 M=|\det\Lambda|^{-1/12}
   =\left(\prod_{j=1}^{6}\sigma_j\right)^{1/6}.
\]
Five standard deviations of \(10^{-4}\) and one of \(10^{-1}\) give
\[
 M=\left((10^{-4})^5\,10^{-1}\right)^{1/6}
   \approx3.1623\times10^{-4}.
\]
This is below P's \emph{illustrative} threshold \(10^{-3}\), despite
a comparatively unconstrained direction.
It is a normalized example, not measured Aero covariance or a
conversion between metres and radians.
See \url{ada/research-note.md}, lines 23--35, and
\url{ada/local-model-run-001.log}, lines 26--27.
\ledger{7,11}

Passive relative-pose statistics can also be compatible with an
externally moved object followed by the hand or an object moved by the
hand.
They do not alone establish the direction of influence.
This qualifies the causal interpretation of the statistic without
invalidating P's task-specific heuristic.
The latest investigation makes the observational qualification
explicit.
\ledger{7,43,48}

\subsection{Retaining a relative-motion constraint}

In compatible local coordinates, write
\[
 \delta x_h=J_h\delta a,
 \qquad
 \delta x_f=J_f\delta a+v_f^{\mathrm{ext}}\Delta t.
\]
Here \(\delta a\) collects wrist and tendon-command increments;
\(J_f\) is an action-response derivative, not Q's joint-to-cable map.
For desired relative increment \(b_f\), define
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 H_f&=J_h-J_f,\\
 e_f(\delta a)&=H_f\delta a-v_f^{\mathrm{ext}}\Delta t-b_f,\\
 \phi_f(\delta a)&\propto
 \exp\!\left[-\tfrac12e_f(\delta a)^\top
                         \Lambda_f e_f(\delta a)\right].
 \end{aligned}
\]
A quadratic actuator prior of precision \(R\succ0\) gives Hessian
\[
 \mathcal H=R+\sum_f H_f^\top\Lambda_fH_f.
\]
Rank-deficient factors leave motions for the prior or other factors to
determine.
Rigid-pose implementations require the appropriate adjoints and
logarithm Jacobians.
\ledger{3,7}

An exogenous frame has \(J_f=0\).
A rigidly carried frame has \(J_f=J_h\), so its factor does not resist
common motion; a requested nonzero relative displacement remains
infeasible.
For the illustrative within-grasp structure
\[
 \delta a=
 \begin{bmatrix}\delta x_h\\\delta u\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 J_h=\begin{bmatrix}I&0\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 J_f=\begin{bmatrix}I&G\end{bmatrix},
\]
one obtains
\[
 H_f=\begin{bmatrix}0&-G\end{bmatrix}.
\]
The factor constrains tendon-induced motion while freeing common wrist
motion.
Deleting it loses that constraint.
See \url{ada/research-note.md}, lines 37--72.
\ledger{3,7}

The checked scalar example uses object displacement \(x+2u\), desired
relative displacement \(0.2\), and desired wrist displacement \(1\):
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 L_{\mathrm{relative}}
   &=10^4(-2u-0.2)^2+(x-1)^2+0.01u^2,\\
 L_{\mathrm{exogenous}}
   &=10^4(x-0.2)^2+(x-1)^2+0.01u^2,\\
 L_{\mathrm{deleted}}
   &=(x-1)^2+0.01u^2.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The relative solution has \(x=1\),
\(u\approx-0.099999975\), and relative residual about
\(-5\times10^{-8}\).
The exogenous approximation gives \(x\approx0.20008,u=0\);
deletion gives \(x=1,u=0\).
Both miss the relative target by \(0.2\).
The small residual in the first solution comes from regularization.
See \url{ada/local-model-run-001.log}, lines 28--42.
\ledger{4}

\begin{objection}
This example supplies the useful relative constraint by construction,
and does not compare against P's complete controller.
\end{objection}

P predicts end-effector poses and leaves gripper actions aside.
An extension to tendon actions would need to be specified.
Moreover, factor pullback through known or learned transformations is
prior art.
The example remains a local illustration, not evidence of a new fusion
principle or a DynaMAC failure.
\ledger{7,8,9,11}

\subsection{Q does not supply the required response model}

Q's map relates joint angles to cable travel, not actuator commands to
object motion.
Even imposing the illustrative equality
\(q_{\mathrm{pip}}=q_{\mathrm{dip}}\), its coefficients admit
\[
 \begin{bmatrix}12.49&7.32&9.00\end{bmatrix}
 \begin{bmatrix}16.32\\-12.49\\-12.49\end{bmatrix}=0.
\]
The interior postures
\[
 q^{(0)}=
 \begin{bmatrix}0.8\\0.5\\0.5\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 q^{(1)}=
 \begin{bmatrix}0.9632\\0.3751\\0.3751\end{bmatrix}
\]
both give \(18.152\) mm cable travel.
This illustrates the rank deficiency already stated by Q.
It does not prove identical equilibria under the same load, or
impossibility of inference from temporal histories and contact dynamics.
See \url{ada/local-model-run-001.log}, lines 3--25.
\ledger{5,9,11}

The original experiment proposed privileged simulation state and paired
actuator probes before attempting identification from tracked object
motion and encoders.
That additional sensing and calibration must be counted.
Q's restricted posture representation does not ensure that changing
contacts preserve it.
A second hand was not needed for the proposed test.
The initial comparison with temporal masking, soft weights, ordinary
relative feedback and existing model-aware fusion was deferred until a
consequential regime could be established.
\ledger{5,7,8,9,11}

\section{Retained investigation: closed-loop frame fusion}

\subsection{Specified local dynamics}

Freeze the Gaussian factors and response in a local Euclidean chart:
\[
 x_{f,t}=Jx_t+Gu_t+c.
\]
Let the retained expert have mean \(x_{f,t}+r\) and precision
\(F\succ0\), and combine the competing experts into mean \(v\) and
precision \(L\succ0\).
Define
\[
 H=F+L,\qquad W=H^{-1}F,\qquad h_t=Gu_t+c+r.
\]
Minimizing the instantaneous quadratic
\[
 \tfrac12\|y-x_{f,t}-r\|_F^2+
 \tfrac12\|y-v\|_L^2
\]
gives
\[
 y_t=WJx_t+Wh_t+(I-W)v.
\]
For the stipulated tracker \(x_{t+1}=(1-\eta)x_t+\eta y_t\),
with \(0<\eta\le1\),
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 x_{t+1}&=A_\eta x_t+b_t,\\
 A_\eta&=(1-\eta)I+\eta WJ,\\
 b_t&=\eta\{Wh_t+(I-W)v\}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
This is an algebraic response model and an assumed execution law,
not P's complete policy or Q's contact dynamics.
See \url{ada/closed-loop-note.md}, lines 19--49.
\ledger{13,18,19}

If \(u_t=K_ux_t+k\), the effective response is \(J+GK_u\).
An independently evolving auxiliary controller requires an augmented
state.
Q's measured tendon dynamics do not identify the wrist gain \(\eta\).
\ledger{18,19,21}

For constant inputs, convergence requires \(\rho(A_\eta)<1\).
When \(I-WJ\) is invertible, put
\[
 \delta=(J-I)v+Gu+c+r.
\]
Then
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 x_\infty-v&=(I-WJ)^{-1}W\delta,\\
 e_N&=A_\eta^Ne_0+
       \eta\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}A_\eta^kW\delta,
 \qquad e_t=x_t-v.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The fixed point is independent of positive scalar tracker gain.
Changing phase means adds forcing; changing matrices requires products
of transition matrices.
Pointwise stable spectra do not prove arbitrary switching stability
or small transient errors.
See \url{ada/closed-loop-note.md}, lines 51--65.
\ledger{19}

\subsection{Rigid attachment: an exact finite-phase bound}

For \(J=I,u=0,r=-c\), the bias vanishes.
If \(\beta_k\) are the generalized eigenvalues of \((F,L)\), then
\[
 \alpha_k=\frac{\beta_k}{1+\beta_k}
\]
are the eigenvalues of \(W\).
Since \(HW=F\), \(W\) is self-adjoint in the \(H\) metric, and
\[
 \sup_{e_0\ne0}\frac{\|e_N\|_H}{\|e_0\|_H}
 =
 \max_k\left[1-\frac{\eta}{1+\beta_k}\right]^N.
\]
Thus the exact worst-mode condition for residual at most
\(0<\epsilon<1\) after \(N\) steps is
\[
 \beta_{\max}\le
 \frac{\eta}{1-\epsilon^{1/N}}-1.
\]
If the right side is negative, even deletion cannot meet that deadline
under the assumed tracker.
\ledger{13,15,18}

For \(N=100,\epsilon=0.05,\eta=0.5\), the bound is approximately
\(15.941658\).
With ideal execution and precision ratio \(1000\), only \(9.51\%\)
of a unit displacement is completed in 100 steps, despite convergence
to the correct asymptotic target.
Practical collapse can therefore be finite-phase lag rather than
permanent freezing.
Capping generalized precision modes can enforce this restricted
deadline condition; it is a performance rule, not a contact classifier.
See \url{ada/closed-loop-note.md}, lines 67--90, and
\url{ada/closed-loop-run-001.log}, lines 3--8.
\ledger{13,15,18,19}

\subsection{Partial coupling: convergence can be biased}

For scalar response \(j\), ratio \(\beta=F/L\), and calibration
\[
 Gu+c+r=(1-j)x_0,
\]
the fixed point and tracker eigenvalue are
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 x_\infty-x_0&=
 \frac{v-x_0}{1+\beta(1-j)},\\
 \lambda_\eta&=
 1-\eta+\eta\frac{\beta j}{1+\beta}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
For \(0\le j<1\), weaker coupling can accelerate convergence while
increasing displacement bias.
At \(\beta=1000,j=0.8\), the equilibrium completes about \(0.498\%\)
of the requested displacement.
Whether that is an error depends on whether the relative constraint
is part of the task.
A modewise extension requires compatible eigenbases.
See \url{ada/closed-loop-note.md}, lines 92--104.
\ledger{15,19}

\subsection{The free-matrix counterexample and its limitation}

The original example specified
\[
 F=\begin{bmatrix}0.9&11.25\\11.25&145\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 L=\begin{bmatrix}0.1&1.25\\1.25&55\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 J=\begin{bmatrix}0.5&0.4\\0.4&0.5\end{bmatrix}.
\]
Both precisions are positive definite, with determinant \(3.9375\).
The response is a Euclidean contraction with eigenvalues \(0.1,0.9\),
but
\[
 W=\begin{bmatrix}0.9&10\\0&0.1\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 WJ=\begin{bmatrix}4.45&5.36\\0.04&0.05\end{bmatrix},
\]
and
\[
 \rho(WJ)=
 \frac{4.5+\sqrt{20.2176}}2
 \approx4.498199279.
\]
With compatible zero targets, arbitrarily small local perturbations
grow.
The positive tracker eigenvalue
\(1+\eta(4.498199279-1)\) remains above one for every \(\eta>0\).
The cause is incompatible metrics, not incompatible targets.
A sufficient common-metric condition is
\[
 \|W\|_H\|J\|_H<1,
\]
which holds in particular if \(J^\top HJ\preceq H\).
See \url{ada/closed-loop-note.md}, lines 106--139.
\ledger{14,18,19}

Review 21 established that
\[
 F^{-1}-(I-J)L^{-1}(I-J)^\top
\]
has a negative diagonal entry, about \(-2.14\).
It cannot be an independent-noise covariance for demonstrations
generated by that \(J\).
The example therefore proves only a free-matrix statement.
\ledger{21,22,28}

The earlier auxiliary construction,
\[
 x\sim\mathcal N(0,L^{-1}),\qquad
 e\sim\mathcal N(0,F^{-1}),\qquad
 Gu=(I-J)x-e,
\]
realizes the covariances through \(x_f=x-e\), but coordinated input makes
the demonstrated effective response the identity.
Freezing \(u\) changes the response at execution.
This is not an unchanged-deployment counterexample.
See \url{ada/consistent-feedback-note.md}, lines 11--17.
\ledger{19,21,28}

\subsection{The determinant does not certify feedback performance}

P's absolute score
\[
 M=\det(F)^{-1/(2d)}
\]
contains neither competing precision, response, execution rate nor
phase duration.
Scaling \(F,L\) by the same \(\kappa>0\) leaves \(W\) unchanged but
multiplies \(M\) by \(\kappa^{-1/2}\).
For a fixed nonsingular diagonal weighting \(D_w\),
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \det(D_wFD_w)&=\det(D_w)^2\det(F),\\
 M_{D_w}&=|\det D_w|^{-1/d}M.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Such weighting only rescales the threshold; it cannot independently
select position and rotation directions through the determinant.
Determinant-one changes leave the score unchanged.
These are limitations of a feedback certificate, not automatic errors
in attachment labels.
See \url{ada/closed-loop-note.md}, lines 145--158.
\ledger{11,13,15,19}

Independent instantaneous quadratic solves agreed with the recurrence
in 100 randomized checks to about \(4\times10^{-15}\).
Fixed-point, finite-horizon, scalar, determinant and reciprocal cases
also passed.
These checks validate the specified model, not MiDiGaP fitting.
See \url{ada/check_closed_loop.py} and
\url{ada/closed-loop-run-001.log}, lines 1--37.
\ledger{14,19,21}

\section{Retained investigation: demonstration-consistent feedback}

\subsection{A constrained covariance model}

Suppose demonstrations satisfy
\[
 x_f=Jx+c+n,\qquad
 \operatorname{Cov}(x)=S\succ0,\qquad
 \operatorname{Cov}(n)=N_0\succeq0,\qquad
 \operatorname{Cov}(x,n)=0.
\]
Fit both experts from this population:
\[
 L=S^{-1},
 \qquad
 F^{-1}=(I-J)S(I-J)^\top+N_0.
\]
Assume the latter covariance is positive definite.
Whitening gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 B&=S^{-1/2}JS^{1/2},&
 a&=I-B,\\
 N&=S^{-1/2}N_0S^{-1/2},&
 C&=aa^\top+N.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The feedback is similar to
\[
 K=(I+C)^{-1}B.
\]
This constrains the precisions and response jointly.
It still describes independent marginal fitting and a frozen observed
frame, not a normalized model-aware product.
See \url{ada/consistent-feedback-note.md}, lines 19--49.
\ledger{23,28}

\subsection{Sharp bound and sufficient stability}

\begin{claim}
Every eigenvalue of \(K\) satisfies
\[
 |\lambda|\le\frac{1+\sqrt2}{2}.
\]
The bound is sharp over the stated class.
If \(a+a^\top\succeq0\), then \(|\lambda|\le1\);
invertible \(a\) makes this sufficient regime strictly stable.
\end{claim}

For a complex unit eigenvector \(z\), put
\(s=\|a^\top z\|_2\).
Then
\[
 \lambda=
 \frac{1-z^*az}{1+s^2+z^*Nz},
\]
so
\[
 |\lambda|\le\frac{1+s}{1+s^2}
 \le\frac{1+\sqrt2}{2}.
\]
Scalar \(B=\sqrt2,N=0\) attains the bound.
That sharpness example is expansive; it does not prove sharpness under
physical-coordinate contraction.
If \(a+a^\top\succeq0\), then
\[
 |1-z^*az|^2\le1+s^2,
 \qquad
 |\lambda|\le
 \frac{\sqrt{1+s^2}}{1+s^2+z^*Nz}\le1.
\]
Strictness follows when \(s>0\) or \(z^*Nz>0\).
The simpler condition \(\|B\|_2<1\) also suffices.
The relevant metric is the demonstration-covariance metric.
See \url{ada/consistent-feedback-note.md}, lines 51--73.
\ledger{23,28}

Tracker eigenvalues are \(1-\eta+\eta\lambda\).
A real eigenvalue above one survives every positive scalar gain;
slowing execution can damp negative near-unit modes while slowing
positive modes.
\ledger{23,25,28}

\subsection{An unchanged-response counterexample}

Take
\[
 B=\begin{bmatrix}
 0.9&1&-2\\0&0.9&1\\0&0&0.9
 \end{bmatrix},
 \qquad N=0,
\]
which gives
\[
 C=\begin{bmatrix}
 5.01&-2.1&0.2\\
 -2.1&1.01&-0.1\\
 0.2&-0.1&0.01
 \end{bmatrix}.
\]
The eigenvalues of \(K\) are approximately
\[
 0.086139827,\qquad1.098360181,\qquad1.001925465.
\]
For \(D=I+C\), exact rational arithmetic gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \det\!\left(\frac{21}{20}D-B\right)
   &=-\frac{137863839}{8000000000}<0,\\
 \det\!\left(\frac{11}{10}D-B\right)
   &=\frac{1253931}{1000000000}>0.
 \end{aligned}
\]
A real growing eigenvalue lies between \(1.05\) and \(1.1\).
Small independent noise preserves instability by continuity.
\ledger{23,28}

Let
\[
 T=\operatorname{diag}(1,10,100),\qquad S=TT^\top.
\]
The physical response is
\[
 J=TBT^{-1}
 =\begin{bmatrix}
 0.9&0.1&-0.02\\0&0.9&0.1\\0&0&0.9
 \end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 \|J\|_2\approx0.966974583.
\]
Drawing \(x\sim\mathcal N(0,S)\), setting \(x_f=Jx\), and fitting both
experts from these same observations produces the unstable feedback
without auxiliary inputs or a response change.
The covariance condition number is \(10000\).
A common scale can localize the cloud without changing the feedback;
the whitened 50-step induced norm is about \(249.11\).
Neither this large linear-system amplification nor physical-coordinate
contraction establishes a realizable contact regime.
See \url{ada/consistent-feedback-note.md}, lines 75--105, and
\url{ada/consistent-fits-001.log}, lines 1--34.
\ledger{21,23,28,30}

\subsection{Few-shot fitting, priors and transients}

The six-dimensional generator adds benign response coordinates of
gain \(0.3\), uses
\[
 T=\operatorname{diag}(0.01,0.1,1,0.1,0.1,0.1),
 \qquad S=TT^\top,
\]
and independent frame-noise covariance \(0.01^2S\).
Both streams use the same observations.
For 300 replicates per cell, the equal isotropic covariance prior is
\[
 R=\gamma\,\frac{\operatorname{tr}(S)}6I.
\]
Selected results are
\[
\begin{array}{c c c c c}
 \text{Demos}&\gamma&\text{Growing fraction}
 &\text{Median }\rho&\text{Median }\|K^{50}\|_2\\
 \hline
 5&10^{-6}&0.740&1.00373&2.256\\
 5&10^{-4}&0.480&0.99964&1.671\\
 5&10^{-2}&0&0.97640&0.489\\
 100&10^{-6}&1.000&1.09533&221.396\\
 100&10^{-4}&1.000&1.04600&49.318\\
 100&10^{-2}&0&0.97381&0.403
\end{array}
\]
Errors use true demonstration-whitened coordinates.
More samples recover the unstable population behavior under weak
regularization; strong regularization removes it in this generator.
A propagated prior \((I-J)R(I-J)^\top\) was also checked, but requires
response information and does not remove finite noisy cross terms.
\ledger{24,28}

The control \(J=0.7I\) produced no unstable eigenvalues in the tested
cells.
Weakly regularized five-sample fits nevertheless had median peak
50-step amplification \(18.47\), with maximum \(48.82\), before decay.
Transient amplification differs from spectral instability.
See \url{ada/consistent-feedback-note.md}, lines 107--128, and
\url{ada/check_consistent_fits.py}.
\ledger{24,28}

These are sample-covariance surrogates, not MiDiGaP.
Trajectory fitting can pool information over time, so five
demonstrations need not mean five effective covariance samples.
At the recorded check, P's website promised a release and its linked
repository was empty:
\url{https://dynamac.cs.uni-freiburg.de/},
\url{https://github.com/robot-learning-freiburg/DynaMAC}.
Its actual regularization and tracker were unavailable.
\ledger{24,28}

\subsection{Limits of the covariance argument}

If \(R_{xn}=\operatorname{Cov}(x,n)\ne0\), then
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 F^{-1}={}&(I-J)S(I-J)^\top+N_0\\
 &-(I-J)R_{xn}-R_{xn}^\top(I-J)^\top.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Regression yields observational response
\[
 J_{\mathrm{obs}}=J+R_{xn}^\top S^{-1},
\]
which need not equal the interventional response.
Finite samples introduce analogous cross terms.
Demonstrated auxiliary feedback similarly fits \(J+GK_u\), not a
response obtained by freezing \(u\).
\ledger{21,28}

An aggregate competing precision need not be \(S^{-1}\).
For the special case of \(m\) identical world-covariance experts,
\[
 K=(I+mC)^{-1}B,\qquad
 |\lambda|\le\frac{1+\sqrt{1+1/m}}2.
\]
This duplication calculation does not justify treating correlated
experts as independent evidence.
See \url{ada/consistent-feedback-note.md}, lines 130--150.
The latest round investigates that independence objection directly.
\ledger{28,40,42}

\subsection{Reciprocal arms and consistent adjoints}

If reverse relative residuals obey \(e_2=-Ae_1\), shared data and
compatible covariance priors give
\[
 C_2=AC_1A^\top,\qquad
 F_2=A^{-\top}F_1A^{-1}.
\]
Transporting one arm into the other's tangent chart restores shared
relative precision.
With positive anchor precisions, both update blocks contract in a
common metric, proving strict stability of the simultaneous reciprocal
update.
An adjoint change alone is not an instability mechanism.
\ledger{18,25,28}

For equal scalar relative-to-anchor precision ratio \(\beta\), the
modes are
\[
 \lambda_{\mathrm{common}}=\frac{\beta}{1+\beta},
 \qquad
 \lambda_{\mathrm{relative}}=-\frac{\beta}{1+\beta}.
\]
At \(\beta=1000\), these are about \(\pm0.999001\):
slow common motion coexists with alternating relative error.
Positive anchors exclude an exact period-two mode; the anchor-free
limit does not.
Gain \(1/2\) damps the alternating mode while further slowing common
motion.
\ledger{25,28}

All 1000 adjoint-consistent trials passed.
Separate isotropic priors in the original charts break congruence:
259 of 1000 five-sample cases at prior scale \(10^{-6}\) were weakly
unstable, with maximum radius \(1.00025745\), about \(1.3\%\) modal
growth in 50 steps.
No growing cases occurred with ten or one hundred samples in that
generator.
See \url{ada/consistent-feedback-note.md}, lines 152--171,
\url{ada/consistent-fits-001.log}, lines 86--88, and
\url{ada/reciprocal-fits-001.log}, lines 1--9.
\ledger{25,28}

\section{Retained investigation: structured responses}

\subsection{Noiseless two-dimensional stability}

Write
\[
 D=I+aa^\top+N,\qquad K=D^{-1}B,\qquad a=I-B.
\]
For \(N=0\),
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 D-B&=a(I+a^\top),\\
 \det(I-K)&=\frac{\det(a)\det(I+a)}{\det D},\\
 \det K&=\frac{\det B}{\det D}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
If real \(B\) is Schur, both \(\det(I-B)\) and \(\det(2I-B)\) are
positive.
In two dimensions, therefore,
\(\det(I-K)>0\) and \(|\det K|<1\).
\ledger{30,32,38}

An eigenvalue at or below \(-1\) is excluded separately.
The earlier eigenvector identity gives, even for \(N\succeq0\),
\[
 \operatorname{Re}\lambda
 \ge\frac{1-\sqrt2}{2}>-1.
\]
For a negative numerator this follows by minimizing
\((1-s)/(1+s^2)\); nonnegative numerators already satisfy the bound.
Complex conjugate eigenvalues in two dimensions have squared modulus
\(\det K<1\).
Real eigenvalues above one must occur together because
\(\det(I-K)>0\), but their product would violate \(|\det K|<1\).
Equality at one is also excluded.
Thus every real two-dimensional Schur response gives Schur feedback
under the noiseless consistent model.
\ledger{32,38}

More generally, real eigenvalues above one occur in an even count,
with multiplicity, for noiseless real Schur \(B\).
For the preceding three-dimensional example,
\[
 \det(I-K)=\frac{0.11^3}{\det(I+C)},
\]
consistent with its pair of growing real modes.
These results do not extend automatically to arbitrary noise.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 51--77.
\ledger{30,32,38}

\subsection{Idempotent articulation responses}

Suppose \(J^2=J\).
Whitening preserves idempotence, so in an appropriate orthonormal basis
\[
 a=\begin{bmatrix}I&T\\0&0\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 B=\begin{bmatrix}0&-T\\0&I\end{bmatrix}.
\]
The zero-noise limiting feedback is
\[
 K=\begin{bmatrix}
 0&-(2I+TT^\top)^{-1}T\\
 0&I
 \end{bmatrix}.
\]
Its spectrum consists of zeros and ones with the appropriate
multiplicities.
This is a limit of positive-covariance fusion, not inversion of a
singular relative covariance.
\ledger{32,38}

For noise \(N=\nu I\) in demonstration-whitened coordinates, the nonzero
eigenvalues become \(1/(1+\nu)\).
Tracker modes are
\[
 1-\frac{\eta\nu}{1+\nu},
\]
along with \(1-\eta\).
Small aligned noise gives slow common motion.
Physical sensor isotropy is not the same as isotropy in this metric.
\ledger{32,38}

For general block noise, define
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 H_p&=2I+TT^\top+N_{11},&
 E_p&=N_{12},\\
 Q_p&=I+N_{22}-E_p^\top H_p^{-1}E_p.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The potentially nonzero eigenvalues are those of
\[
 Q_p^{-1}(I+E_p^\top H_p^{-1}T),
\]
which need not be contractive.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 79--113.
\ledger{32,38}

For the physical orthogonal projection \(J=\operatorname{diag}(0,1)\),
write \(S=[s_{ij}]\), \(N_0=[n_{ij}]\).
The nonzero feedback eigenvalue exceeds one exactly when
\[
 n_{12}(s_{12}+n_{12})>
 (2s_{11}+n_{11})n_{22}.
\]
Indeed,
\[
 C_0=\operatorname{diag}(s_{11},0)+N_0,\qquad
 W=S(S+C_0)^{-1},
\]
and the nonzero eigenvalue of \(WJ\) is \(W_{22}\).
The example
\[
 S=\begin{bmatrix}1&0.8\\0.8&1\end{bmatrix},
 \qquad
 N_0=0.1\begin{bmatrix}9&3\\3&1\end{bmatrix}
\]
has spectrum \(\{0,101/99\}\), positive expert covariances, and
\(\operatorname{cond}(S)=9\).
Noise is independent of state but correlated across coordinates.
Its trace is half the state trace variance.
Any positive replacement of \(0.1\) retains growth, tending to zero
growth as that scale vanishes.
A sufficiently small positive isotropic noise floor preserves it.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 115--128, and
\url{ada/structured-followup-001.log}, lines 23--33.
\ledger{35,37,38}

\subsection{Compliance and metric alignment}

For symmetric positive stiffnesses,
\[
 J=(K_c+K_e)^{-1}K_c,\qquad H_s=K_c+K_e,
\]
the matrix \(J_H=H_s^{1/2}JH_s^{-1/2}\) is symmetric with spectrum in
\((0,1)\).
This does not ensure contraction in the demonstration metric.
Let
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 S_H&=H_s^{1/2}SH_s^{1/2},&
 \kappa&=\operatorname{cond}(S_H),\\
 m&=\lambda_{\min}(J_H),&
 M&=\lambda_{\max}(J_H),\\
 c&=(M+m)/2,& d&=(M-m)/2.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Writing \(J_H=cI+E_H\), with \(\|E_H\|_2\le d\), and using
\[
 \|S_H^{1/2}z\|_2\|S_H^{-1/2}z\|_2
 \le\frac{\sqrt\kappa+1/\sqrt\kappa}{2}
\]
for unit \(z\), gives
\[
 \lambda_{\max}\!\left(\frac{B+B^\top}{2}\right)
 \le c+\frac d2\left(\sqrt\kappa+\frac1{\sqrt\kappa}\right).
\]
If the right side is at most one, the accretivity result proves strict
stability for every \(N_0\succeq0\).
For \(d>0\), with \(q=(1-c)/d\), an explicit sufficient condition is
\[
 \kappa\le\left(q+\sqrt{q^2-1}\right)^2.
\]
This is conservative, not an optimal anisotropy threshold.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 130--152.
\ledger{34,38}

Conversely, a growing mode requires
\[
 -\operatorname{Re}(z^*az)>
 \frac{s^2(1+s^2)}2,\qquad
 s=\|a^\top z\|_2<1.
\]
Compare
\[
 |1-z^*az|^2\le1-2\operatorname{Re}(z^*az)+s^2
\]
with the denominator squared, at least \((1+s^2)^2\).
The inequality also implies \(s<1\).
Thus the numerical range of \(B\) must extend beyond one.
Physical-coordinate contraction need not prevent this after whitening.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 154--161.
\ledger{30,38}

\subsection{A diagonal-spring counterexample}

Take
\[
 J=\operatorname{diag}\left(\frac{999}{1000},\frac56,\frac1{100}\right),
\]
and
\[
 S=\frac1{1000}
 \begin{bmatrix}
 767&-1023&-681\\
 -1023&1456&1029\\
 -681&1029&777
 \end{bmatrix}.
\]
The covariance eigenvalues are approximately
\(0.00351279,0.09098815,2.90549907\), giving condition number \(827.12\).
Positive diagonal spring pairs
\[
 K_c=100J,\qquad K_e=100(I-J)
\]
realize this response.
Their diagonal stiffnesses are approximately
\[
 K_c:(99.9,83.33,1),\qquad K_e:(0.1,16.67,99).
\]
These are constructed values, not Q measurements.
\ledger{34,38}

At \(N_0=0\), feedback eigenvalues are approximately
\[
 1.0765501,\qquad1.0001559,\qquad0.0002405.
\]
For \(C_0=(I-J)S(I-J)^\top\), exact arithmetic gives opposite signs of
\[
 p(\lambda)=\det[\lambda(S+C_0)-JS]
\]
at \(21/20\) and \(11/10\).
Since \(WJ\) is similar to \((S+C_0)^{-1}JS\), this certifies a growing
real mode.
A Jordan block and a deployment response change are unnecessary.
Failed searches with covariance condition-number caps \(4,16,100\)
are not exclusion theorems or evidence about real demonstrations.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 163--176, and
\url{ada/structured-followup-001.log}, lines 1--22.
\ledger{34,38}

\subsection{Earlier controlled rollouts and the deletion objection}

The spring rollout uses phase-varying sinusoidal means, a 20 Hz
gain-\(0.5\) tracker, same-demonstration covariance fits, and a prior
propagated through \(I-J\).
At covariance scale \(0.02\) m, the retained tracker radius is
\(1.038275\), while the score is \(0.343\) mm.
Requiring five consecutive samples below the illustrative \(1\) mm
cutoff deletes the frame and prevents growth.
Persistent-mask RMS is \(0.494\) mm; immediate deletion, the
relative-precision rule, response-based deletion and model-aware fusion
all give about \(0.346\) mm.
The recorded 5-, 30- and 100-demonstration draws give the same
qualitative outcome.
Extreme unmasked positions reflect a globally linear surrogate,
not a plausible robot workspace.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 178--186, and
\url{ada/structured-followup-001.log}, lines 35--58.
\ledger{34,38}

The noisy slider scales covariances by \(10^{-4}\), uses a \(1\) cm
sinusoidal target, independent observations every \(50\) ms, gain
\(0.5\), and 300 replicates per condition.
All controllers receive the same fitted covariances and known \(J\).
The final run adds noise floor \(10^{-7}I\) and prior
\[
 R=10^{-6}\frac{\operatorname{tr}(S)}2I.
\]
Sample means are supplied exactly.
The population score is \(5.65\) mm, so the persistent \(1\) mm mask
never removes the frame.
Retained fusion crosses \(20\) mm error in 266/300 trials over 10 s,
with median crossing time \(3.325\) s among crossings.
\ledger{37,38}

The crossing fractions are
\[
\begin{array}{c c c c c c}
 \text{Fit}&\text{Score}&\text{Spectrum}&
 \text{Relative}&\text{Model}&\text{Delete}\\
 \hline
 \text{Population}&0.887&0&0&0&0\\
 5&0.673&0.123&0.033&0.070&0\\
 30&0.843&0.153&0.003&0&0\\
 100&0.880&0.077&0&0&0
\end{array}
\]
``Spectrum'' deletes when the fitted tracker radius is at least one.
``Relative'' uses the 50-step, \(5\%\) rigid deadline rule, only a
heuristic for this projection.
Population median RMS is \(21.8\) mm for retention or score masking,
\(0.473\) mm for model-aware fusion, and \(0.305\) mm for deletion.
Five-demonstration model-aware RMS is \(1.66\) mm.
Stable homogeneous spectra can still permit large noise-driven errors.
The log's instability fields describe the unmasked candidate, not
every controller named on the same row.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 188--209, and
\url{ada/projection-rollout-002.log}, lines 1--24.
\ledger{37,38}

\begin{objection}
Neither earlier task needed the retained frame: the world mean fully
specified the desired motion.
\end{objection}

Deletion matched or beat the retained methods.
These experiments therefore established no advantage over adequate
frame removal.
That comparative objection was accepted in review 40 and motivated
the informative-reference experiment appended below.
The historical result remains valid; it is no longer a description of
every experiment in the record.
\ledger{34,37,38,40,44,48}

The structured checks passed 3000 two-dimensional Schur cases,
1000 idempotent block-spectrum cases, and 3000 alignment-bound cases.
The largest radius in the two-dimensional checks was about
\(0.9999973\).
These checks support, rather than replace, the proofs.
See \url{ada/check_structured_theorems.py} and
\url{ada/structured-theorems-001.log}, lines 1--3.
The rollout scripts, arrays and failed attempts remain recorded in
\url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 234--236.
\ledger{32,38}

\section{Retained Aero diagnostics and corrected scope}

The covariance diagnostic used the official checkpoint, pinned assets,
an allowlisted array reader and a NumPy deterministic actor.
Recorded sources were:
\begin{itemize}
\item
\url{https://github.com/Chestnut-Robotics/aero-hand-open/tree/7bb2f23485a7d440960c21046da642de132b7745}
\item
\url{https://github.com/google-deepmind/mujoco_playground/tree/1bd244fa9c51fa7074c7a449934f556d898f7a15}
\item
\url{https://github.com/Chestnut-Robotics/aero-open-sim/tree/dfeff43507fe82f515fed81592070e39a9bb92f6}
\end{itemize}
It used plain MuJoCo 3.3.7, a \(50\) mm, \(69.2\) g cube,
\(0.01\) s integration and \(0.05\) s control, without observation
noise or parameter randomization.
It was not the original MJX/Brax runtime.
See \url{ada/closed-loop-note.md}, lines 166--174.
\ledger{16,19}

The pinned training history convention adds a delay relative to
deployment.
The training-style nominal smoke survived 15 s; deployment settings
dropped after 1.15 s.
Several settings changed together, so the cause was not isolated.
Neither reproduces Q's quantitative evaluation.
\ledger{16,19}

Each covariance cohort comprised 32 local-reset trials of at most 5 s.
Hand-angle perturbations had standard deviation \(s\), cube position
\(0.1s\) metres, and cube rotation \(s\) radians.
The diagnostic computed phase-conditioned palm-in-cube sample
covariances in local rotation coordinates, using metres and radians
without regularization.
This is a proxy, not P's fitted stream, segmentation or temporal filter.
\ledger{16,19}

Deployment-style \(s=0.01\) trials had 11/32 survivors and no flags before
the first fall at 1.10 s.
Training-style \(s=0.01\) had 31/32 survivors and no flags.
At \(s=0.001\), 30/32 survived, with flags only at \(0,0.05,0.10\) s.
Successful-only checks reproduced this pattern, with median scores
about \(0.155\) and \(0.173\).
The briefly flagged interval included about \(0.16\) rad mean relative
rotation; temporal filtering might remove it but was not tested.
The initial score scales from \(2.79\times10^{-3}\) to
\(2.79\times10^{-4}\) when \(s\) decreases tenfold.
Later broad orientation dispersion weakens the local Gaussian
interpretation.
See \url{ada/aero-summary-001.log}, lines 1--3, and
\url{ada/aero-covariance-004.log} and
\url{ada/aero-covariance-005.log}, lines 33--35.
\ledger{16,19,21,28}

The moving-wrist follow-up prescribed a mocap mount, retained the tendon
policy, and used paired probes from identical contact states at phases
\(0.5,1,2,3\) s and horizons \(0.05,0.2\) s.
Rotation used a \(0.05\) m characteristic length.
Initial amplitudes were \(10^{-4}\) and \(5\times10^{-5}\) m in those
coordinates; 64 random perturbations tested each local fit.
The response retained the nominal palm-to-cube transform, including
its lever arm.
These were calibration probes, not task demonstrations or arm dynamics.
\ledger{26,28}

The first attempt failed on an unavailable copying binding.
The corrected run preserved grasp over short horizons, but halving
amplitude changed derivatives by \(96\%\)--\(214\%\), and
independent-direction errors were \(100\%\)--\(198\%\).
Formal fusion radii \(0.087\)--\(0.691\) therefore supported no
closed-loop conclusion.
The score crossed the illustrative cutoff only at phase \(0.5\) s over
the short horizon, with probe-dependent scale.
See \url{ada/aero-wrist-probes-001.log} and
\url{ada/aero-wrist-probes-002.log}.
\ledger{26,28}

Refinement changed both solver iterations from five to 100 and
amplitude to \(10^{-5}\).
Only two of eight conditions validated.
At phases 2 and 3 s over the short horizon, derivative changes were
\(0.00093\%\) and \(0.00137\%\), and prediction errors \(0.60\%\) and
\(0.31\%\); formal radii were \(0.464,0.719\).
The phase-2 long-horizon fit had radius \(1.021\) but about \(100\%\)
prediction error and cannot support instability.
See \url{ada/aero-wrist-probes-003.log}, lines 5--7.
\ledger{27,28}

The earlier description of this as strengthening a negative physical
result was withdrawn.
The covariances were experimenter-chosen and near-isotropic, with a
small regularizer, tiny probes and non-released solver settings.
The phase-2 short-horizon case already satisfies \(\|B\|_2<1\).
These tests neither establish nor refute the anisotropic-demonstration
regime.
A finite-horizon derivative also need not be an iteratable memoryless
law.
No Aero controller advantage, DynaMAC failure or sensing impossibility
was established.
See \url{ada/structured-feedback-note.md}, lines 230--232.
\ledger{30,33,38,48}

\section{Appended investigation: correlated experts}

\subsection{What review 40 asked to distinguish}

The world and relative residuals in the independent-noise model have
cross-covariance \(S(I-J)^\top\).
Review 40 therefore asked whether growing modes arise from discarding
this correlation rather than from endogeneity alone.
It also requested a normalized-product comparison, a useful external
reference, a few-shot qualification, and a final reassessment rather
than another search for unstable response matrices.
\ledger{40,41}

\subsection{Joint conditioning removes growth under independent noise}

At a fixed phase, let
\[
 f=Jx+c+n,\qquad
 x\sim\mathcal N(m,S),\qquad
 n\sim\mathcal N(0,N_0),\qquad n\perp x,
\]
with \(S\succ0\) and \(JSJ^\top+N_0\succ0\).
The fitted population conditional mean is
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 \pi(f)&=m+G(f-Jm-c),\\
 G&=SJ^\top(JSJ^\top+N_0)^{-1}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The learner can estimate \(G\) directly from paired observations;
the population formula does not require supplying \(J\) to it.
\ledger{42,48}

With \(B=S^{-1/2}JS^{1/2}\) and
\(N=S^{-1/2}N_0S^{-1/2}\),
\[
 S^{-1/2}GJS^{1/2}
 =B^\top(BB^\top+N)^{-1}B.
\]
The Schur complement of the joint covariance gives
\[
 0\preceq B^\top(BB^\top+N)^{-1}B\preceq I.
\]
Thus every population eigenvalue of \(GJ\) lies in \([0,1]\).
Positive definite noise makes the upper inequality strict.
Joint conditioning removes the growing modes of the earlier
independent-noise population examples.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 21--47.
\ledger{42,48}

For tracker gain \(0<\eta\le1\), define
\[
 T=(1-\eta)I+\eta GJ.
\]
When the next phase mean is supplied before computing the target,
\[
 e_{t+1}=T(e_t-\Delta m_t)+\eta Gn_t,
 \qquad
 \Delta m_t=m_{t+1}-m_t.
\]
A mode \(\mu\) of \(GJ\) retains
\((1-\eta+\eta\mu)^H\) of initial error after \(H\) fixed-phase steps.
Nonexpansion is not instantaneous tracking.
\ledger{42,48}

For noiseless invertible \(J\), \(G=J^{-1}\), so \(GJ=I\).
The target is exactly the current state: the phase mean cancels even
when it changes.
The diagonal-spring example's independent-fusion eigenvalues become
unit conditional eigenvalues.
Calibration removes growth but leaves complete self-following.
In a noiseless rank-deficient pseudoinverse limit, the observed
subspace is read back while the mean drives unobserved directions.
These singular limits are not ordinary full-density Gaussian fits.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 49--66.
\ledger{42,48}

\subsection{Retaining residual correlation gives the same controller}

Using \(a=I-J\), the joint residual covariance is
\[
 \Omega=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 S&Sa^\top\\
 aS&aSa^\top+N_0
 \end{bmatrix}.
\]
Independent marginal fusion discards its off-diagonal blocks.
Evaluating the full Gaussian at candidate residuals
\[
 \begin{bmatrix}
 y-m\\
 y-f-(am-c)
 \end{bmatrix}
\]
and normalizing over \(y\) yields the same conditional controller.
The invertible change of variables between \((x,f)\) and
\((x,x-f)\) establishes the equivalence.
\ledger{42,48}

In 1000 random models of dimensions one through eight, the similarity
identity agreed to \(7.4\times10^{-14}\), and the conditional and
correlated-residual gains to \(7.7\times10^{-13}\).
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 68--84, and
\url{ada/correlated-checks-002.log}, line 1.
Within this model, independence miscalibration explains growth while
endogeneity explains remaining self-following.
This is not a theorem about every independence approximation in P.
\ledger{42,48}

\subsection{Joint normalization is a distinct operation}

Pignat's normalized product training does not mean simply placing a
full covariance on stacked residuals.
For centered scalar variables, consider
\[
 q(y\mid f)\propto
 \exp\!\left[-\tfrac12Ly^2-\tfrac12F(y-f)^2\right],
 \qquad L,F\ge0.
\]
Its gain is \(w=F/(L+F)\in[0,1]\).
For observational regression gain
\(g=\operatorname{Cov}(x,f)/\operatorname{Var}(f)\), normalized
conditional maximum likelihood gives
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 w_\star&=\operatorname{clip}(g,0,1),\\
 L+F&=\left[\operatorname{Var}(x-w_\star f)\right]^{-1}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
The intercept is \(\bar x-w_\star\bar f\).
Zero precision permits a constant factor; zero residual variance
requires a singular precision limit.
The scalar optimum was checked against direct residual minimization.
\ledger{45,48}

For known response \(0\le j<1\), the physical relative feature is
\((1-j)y-e\).
The normalized product has external-reference slope
\[
 h=\frac{(1-j)F}{L+(1-j)^2F}
 \in[0,1/(1-j)].
\]
Its optimum clips unrestricted context regression to this interval.
A negative task slope cannot be represented with these physical
features and nonnegative precisions.
Richer learned features or mean models change the family.
\ledger{45,48}

For the earlier noiseless response-aware unconditional model, total
precision is
\[
 H=L+a^\top Fa,\qquad a=I-J.
\]
Normalized maximum likelihood fixes \(H=S^{-1}\), but not the factor
allocation.
For invertible \(a\) and \(C=aSa^\top\), every
\[
 F=\gamma C^{-1},\qquad
 L=(1-\gamma)S^{-1},\qquad 0\le\gamma\le1
\]
gives the same distribution.
Independent marginal fitting instead gives \(H=2S^{-1}\).
Normalization repairs calibration without identifying the generating
mechanism.
This is a scalar or linear-Gaussian specialization, not a full
implementation of Pignat's method.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 86--121.
\ledger{45,47,48}

Likewise, fixed half-half covariance-intersection weights scale both
precisions equally and leave the fused mean unchanged.
A guarantee about uncertainty under correlated estimates is not by
itself a receding-controller stability guarantee.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, line 123.
\ledger{47,48}

\subsection{An informative reference changes the stability question}

Now suppose
\[
 f=jx+e+n,\qquad x=m+Ae+w,
\]
where \(e,w,n\) are independent Gaussian variables with variances
\(E,R,N\).
The controller does not observe \(e\) directly.
Because demonstration state depends on \(e\), the external frame
contribution is correlated with \(x\).
The independent-noise theorem does not apply.
\ledger{43,48}

For noiseless demonstrations, \(R=N=0\), and \(1+jA\ne0\),
\[
 g=\frac{A}{1+jA},\qquad
 gj=\frac{jA}{1+jA}.
\]
The conditional's fixed point has the correct task slope \(A\), but
tracker stability requires
\[
 |1-\eta+\eta gj|<1
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 jA>\eta/2-1.
\]
At \(jA=-1\), the frame is constant in demonstrations and regression
does not identify this gain.
For \(-1<jA<-1/2\), ideal execution oscillates; sufficiently small
positive gain can stabilize it.
For \(jA<-1\), the positive loop gain exceeds one and reducing a
positive execution gain does not stabilize it.
\ledger{43,48}

For desired pose \(d_t=m_t+Ae_t\), the noiseless-fit tracking error obeys
\[
 \epsilon_{t+1}
 =(1-\eta+\eta gj)(\epsilon_t-\Delta d_t)+\eta gn_t.
\]
With finite demonstration or observation noise,
\[
 g=\frac{A(1+jA)E+jR}
          {(1+jA)^2E+j^2R+N}.
\]
When the equilibrium denominator is nonzero,
\[
 \frac{g}{1-jg}
 =A+\frac{jR-AN}{(1+jA)E+N}.
\]
Correct observational regression therefore need not imply either a
correct equilibrium or stable feedback.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 125--169.
\ledger{43,48}

Independently fitted scalar experts use
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 S&=A^2E+R,\\
 C&=((1-j)A-1)^2E+(1-j)^2R+N,\\
 p&=\frac{S}{S+C}.
 \end{aligned}
\]
Their equilibrium slope is \(p/(1-jp)\); deletion has slope zero.
For \(j=0.5,A=0.8,R=N=0\), the joint conditional gives slope \(0.8\),
independent fusion gives \(0.941176\), and deletion gives zero.
For \(A=-1.6\), the conditional has \(g=-8\) and ideal loop gain \(-4\),
despite its correct fixed point.
The normalized measured-frame product clips this gain to zero.
The known-response physical-feature product cannot express the
negative task slope either.
These are designed compensation tasks, not evidence of that sign
structure in P's typical grasps.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 171--181.
\ledger{43,45,48}

\subsection{Observational ambiguity and its temporal qualification}

Two worlds can share the full-rank per-phase covariance
\[
 \operatorname{Cov}
 \begin{pmatrix}x\\f\end{pmatrix}
 =
 \begin{bmatrix}1&-0.25\\-0.25&0.0635\end{bmatrix}.
\]
For each physical \(j\), define \(e=f-jx\) and regress \(x=Ae+w\).
Gaussian regression makes \(w\) independent of \(e\).
The recorded constructions are
\[
\begin{array}{c c c c c}
 j&A&E&R&\lambda_{\eta=0.5}\\
 \hline
 0.2&-2.211302&0.2035&0.004914&0.106299\\
 0.9&-0.868908&1.3235&0.000756&-1.271654
\end{array}
\]
Both have the same fitted gain, approximately \(-3.937008\), but
different physical feedback.
This is per-phase observational ambiguity with a hidden reference,
not a new causal theorem or impossibility result for histories,
additional observations or interventions.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 183--199, and
\url{ada/correlated-checks-002.log}, lines 15--16.
\ledger{43,47,48}

If the reference \(e_i\) is constant within each demonstration and
\(j\) is invariant, time-centering removes it:
\[
 \widehat j=
 \frac{\sum_{i,t}(x_{it}-\bar x_i)(f_{it}-\bar f_i)}
      {\sum_{i,t}(x_{it}-\bar x_i)^2}.
\]
With exact hand-state sensing, independent zero-mean frame noise and
nonzero temporal excitation, this is an ordinary consistent estimate.
Averaging \(f-\widehat jx\) gives each demonstration's reference;
regressing the desired pose on these references estimates its task
dependence.
At execution, use \(\widehat e=f-\widehat jx\).
The tracker coefficient becomes
\[
 1-\eta+\eta\widehat A(j-\widehat j),
\]
approaching \(1-\eta\) as identification improves.
\ledger{43,44,48}

If the reference moves during demonstrations, the estimate acquires
the omitted contribution
\(\operatorname{Cov}_t(x,e)/\operatorname{Var}_t(x)\).
Changing contacts, noisy hand-state measurements and unexcited
directions also invalidate the simple argument.
These conditions are substantial, especially for Q, and the
estimator is not claimed as new.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 201--215.
\ledger{44,47,48}

\subsection{Matched informative-reference rollouts}

The new scalar spring surrogate uses \(k_c=k_e=50\), hence
\(f=0.5x+e\), with external support \(h=2e\).
Controllers observe the same exact hand state and noisy frame, not
the support or \(e\).
Targets are clipped to magnitude \(100\) mm and tracked with gain
\(0.5\) at 20 Hz for 10 s.
The phase mean is a \(5\) mm sinusoid; the external reference is a
signed sinusoid plus a step, with amplitude sampled from \(7\)--\(13\)
mm.
\ledger{44,48}

Each training demonstration has a static reference with standard
deviation \(10\) mm, a fixed hand-offset residual of standard deviation
\(0.5\) mm, and independent frame noise of standard deviation \(0.2\)
mm per timestamp.
Phase means are estimated.
The scalar covariance floor is \(0.01\) square mm.
There are 300 matched fits and noise trials per task slope and
demonstration count.
Each demonstration has 201 timestamps: five demonstrations mean five
independent contexts, not five observed points.
\ledger{44,45,48}

All methods share raw data, initial conditions and observation-noise
sequences.
The joint conditional and independent products fit phase statistics.
Temporal and known-response regressions additionally exploit a
constant reference within each trajectory.
The latter are stronger structural assumptions, so this is not an
ablation that changes only normalization.
The unrestricted context regression has a richer mean family than
the normalized physical-feature product.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 217--225.
\ledger{44,45,48}

For five demonstrations, median tracking RMS in millimetres is
\[
\begin{array}{l r r r}
 \text{Controller}&A=0.8&A=-1.6&A=-3\\
 \hline
 \text{Independent fusion}&0.903&12.072&21.777\\
 \text{Persistent mask}&0.903&12.072&21.777\\
 \text{Delete}&4.586&8.996&17.058\\
 \text{Joint conditional}&0.472&31.832&109.218\\
 \text{Normalized measured frame}&0.472&8.996&28.078\\
 \text{Known response, marginals}&1.062&10.801&20.086\\
 \text{Known response, normalized}&0.336&8.996&17.058\\
 \text{Known response, context}&0.336&0.472&0.791\\
 \text{Temporal identification}&0.336&0.472&0.789
\end{array}
\]
The persistent score is above the illustrative cutoff, so masking is
retention in these trials.
The positive task now establishes a real deletion cost and a benefit
from a demonstration-only retained controller.
The compensation tasks show that calibrated conditioning alone can
fail, with actuator saturation included.
Negative-slope success belongs to unrestricted context regression,
not merely to normalizing the original physical-factor family.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 227--239, and
\url{ada/informative-rollout-002.log}.
\ledger{44,45,48}

The actual known-response normalized product was added separately after
the baseline distinction was identified.
All 96 earlier summary rows were unchanged in the expanded 108-row
comparison, with 32400 method-trial records preserved.
These are synthetic tracking errors, not DynaMAC success rates, Aero
results, sample-efficiency claims or a new method.
\ledger{45,48}

\subsection{Population calibration does not guarantee few-shot safety}

A full joint covariance in 12 dimensions has 78 distinct entries,
versus 42 for separate six-dimensional covariances.
With five phase-aligned samples, the joint sample covariance has rank
at most four; both approaches need structure or regularization.
P's trajectory models may have more effective observations per
component than this simplification assumes.
\ledger{46,48}

For \(J=0.5I,S=I,N_0=0.1I\) in six dimensions, fitted conditionals give
the following fractions with true ideal-loop spectral radius above one:
\[
\begin{array}{c c c c}
 \text{Demos}&10^{-6}I&0.01I&0.1I\\
 \hline
 5&0.627&0.514&0.042\\
 10&0.772&0.560&0.007\\
 100&0&0&0
\end{array}
\]
Each cell uses 1000 independent fits; ridge rows are not paired.
The population factorization fails for a fitted gain when empirical
state--noise cross terms are nonzero.
The nonmonotone small-sample outcomes are not a sample-complexity law.
These are surrogate spectra, not robot failure rates.
See \url{ada/correlated-checks-002.log}, lines 2--10, and
\url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 261--263.
\ledger{46,48}

\section{External sources and the novelty boundary}

The source checks progressively narrowed the claims.
The following papers were inspected through the shared source pool;
the cited line ranges are those recorded by the peers.
No new source retrieval is needed to consolidate their findings.
\ledger{6,8,17,36,47,48}

\begin{itemize}
\item
\emph{Generalized Task-Parameterized Skill Learning},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.01696},
pooled source lines 154--208, already gives precision-weighted
least-squares fusion and confidence-scaled experts.
\ledger{6}

\item
\emph{Relaxed-Rigidity Constraints},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.00942},
lines 130--170, already treats within-grasp motion and directional
relaxation, using a fixed thumb--object transform and joint kinematics.
Mink's relative-frame derivation at
\url{https://kevinzakka.github.io/mink/background/derivations.html}
documents the standard adjoint-correct Jacobian difference.
\ledger{6}

\item
Pignat, Silv\'erio and Calinon,
\emph{Learning from demonstration using products of experts},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.03505},
lines 249--265 and 523--525, includes known or learned task
transformations; lines 841--845 and 929--1037 discuss control and
Jacobian linearization.
Lines 200--223 warn about separately fitted experts, while
269--287 discuss joint normalization and the linear-Gaussian special
case.
This is a required constructive and calibration baseline.
\ledger{8,17,40,47}

\item
Silv\'erio et al.,
\emph{Learning Task Priorities from Demonstrations},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06791},
lines 439--477, uses Jacobian task parameters for Gaussian fusion;
line 1023 assumes known task Jacobians, and line 1288 retains
dimension-wise variability.
\ledger{17}

\item
Elastic-DS,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.01884},
lines 166 and 388, provides Lyapunov-related stability context for
adaptation to given task descriptors, with continuous online adaptation
left open in the inspected discussion.
This does not certify novelty of the present boundaries.
\ledger{17,19}

\item
Perdomo et al., \emph{Performative Prediction},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.06673},
lines 690--750, studies repeated minimization when the distribution
responds to the preceding decision, including contraction conditions
and divergence.
The retained-frame update is its quadratic special case:
\[
 Z\sim\mathcal N(Jx+c,N_0),
\]
\[
 \ell(Z;y)=
 \tfrac12\|y-Z-r\|_F^2+\tfrac12\|y-v\|_L^2.
\]
Minimizing its expectation gives the previously derived update.
The generic feedback mechanism is therefore not new.
\ledger{36,38,47}

\item
\emph{Addressing Ambiguity in Imitation Learning through Product of
Experts based Negative Feedback},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.26467},
lines 94--140, concerns negative demonstrations and sequential failure
avoidance, rather than the instantaneous frame-feedback calculation.
Its inspection does not establish absence of closer prior work.
\ledger{38}

\item
\emph{Correlated Product of Experts},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.09519},
lines 364--372 and 430--491, addresses correlated expert prediction and
covariance intersection in sparse Gaussian-process regression.
It further limits novelty claims for covariance-aware fusion.
\ledger{47}

\item
\emph{Causal Confusion in Imitation Learning},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.11979},
lines 646--658 and 725, treats action-induced observations and
observational ambiguity.
\emph{Feedback in Imitation Learning},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.02872},
lines 349--371, cautions against conflating feedback, model limitations,
covariate shift and causal confounding.
The hidden reference and restriction to per-phase pairs are explicit
assumptions here.
\ledger{47}
\end{itemize}

For the local model, existing response-aware factor evaluation uses
\[
 (I-J)x-Gu-c,
\]
with Hessian
\[
 L+(I-J)^\top F(I-J)\succ0.
\]
It avoids the particular frozen-frame iteration, but does not solve
unknown contacts, execution dynamics or identification automatically.
The earlier baseline implemented this quadratic idea, not Pignat's
complete normalized learning procedure.
The latest scalar comparisons distinguish these operations.
\ledger{8,17,19,45,48}

Repeated pool-helper requests for Silv\'erio 2015 and Jankowski 2021,
including DOI
\url{https://doi.org/10.1109/IROS.2015.7353413}
and
\url{https://doi.org/10.1109/LRA.2021.3061310},
and author-hosted URLs, failed with Semantic Scholar HTTP 429.
The Silv\'erio publication record at
\url{https://publications.idiap.ch/publications/show/3219}
confirms bimanual pose-dynamical-system work, but full-source overlap
remains unresolved.
Indexed Jankowski material concerning probabilistic feedback and
dynamic context was not used to certify novelty.
\ledger{28,36,47,48}

Attempts are preserved in the continuation and round-four/five
pool-fetch logs.
The latest successful new paper requests obtained pooled LaTeX after
an interpreter-related extraction failure.
No separate paper copies or PDF-to-text extraction supplied missing
evidence.
The searches do not prove novelty of the narrower covariance or
mechanical statements.
See \url{ada/correlated-feedback-note.md}, lines 265--277.
\ledger{36,47,48}

\section{Objections resolved and still open}

The original factor-pullback novelty claim is withdrawn.
The encoder null direction illustrates Q's stated rank deficiency,
not a new observability theorem.
The phase-conditioned interpretation of P's covariance and the
illustrative status of its threshold are retained.
\ledger{2,5,6,8,11,48}

The free-matrix demonstration-consistency objection was resolved by
a different counterexample fitted and executed with the same response.
The Jordan-block objection was answered by the diagonal-spring
construction, while the two-dimensional, idempotent and reciprocal
results identify benign restricted classes.
Those calculations stand, but their physical significance and exact
novelty remain uncertain.
\ledger{21,23,25,30,32,34,35,38,40}

The latest round resolves part of the independence objection.
Under independent population frame noise, full joint conditioning
eliminates the growing modes.
It does not eliminate state read-back or finite-phase lag.
With a hidden informative reference, correct observational regression
can again yield unstable feedback.
The observationally identical pair demonstrates why per-phase
calibration is not, by itself, response identification.
These are restricted statements, not a general diagnosis of P.
\ledger{42,43,46,48}

The deletion objection is now resolved within a synthetic informative
task: retaining the frame improves tracking with five demonstrations.
It remains unresolved for the proposed Q/P application.
The successful retained controllers are standard conditioning,
normalized fitting or context regression.
Temporal identification exploits exact state sensing, constant
demonstration references and an invariant response; its signed task
model is richer than the original physical-feature product.
\ledger{44,45,47,48}

The material is still thin as a contribution about this pair.
There is no actual P implementation comparison, Q-specific method,
realistic contact-response identification, hardware transfer result or
sample-efficiency gain.
The Aero diagnostics neither tested the required anisotropic
demonstration regime nor supplied a validated problematic response.
Q motivates the question but is unnecessary for the surviving
theorems and spring experiments.
\ledger{33,38,40,48}

The evidence supports a qualified account of distinct calibration,
motion-generation and identification problems.
It does not support treating all feedback failure as causal
confounding, or claiming joint conditioning as a new remedy.
The final peer recommendation is therefore PAUSE for the original
combined robotics project.
\ledger{47,48,49}

\section{What remains open and the smallest next step}

Open questions are exact novelty of the narrow mathematical statements;
full Silv\'erio/Jankowski overlap; P's actual stream fitting,
regularization and tracking behavior; empirical sensing and
demonstration covariances; and whether a realistic partly controlled
object permits useful response identification from the available
trajectories.
For Aero, object sensing and hidden contact state remain additional
requirements rather than demonstrated advantages.
\ledger{40,47,48}

The previous smallest next step, an informative articulated or
compliant surrogate, has now been completed.
It establishes deletion cost but also shows that familiar statistical
methods suffice under the constructed assumptions.
Another synthetic unstable matrix would not settle the remaining
question.
\ledger{40,44,48}

A continuation should be conditional on access to a concrete partially
coupled task and the actual policy implementation.
The smallest useful test would fit its streams from matched
demonstrations, establish whether the reference contains necessary
external information, and compare P's implemented masking and temporal
checks with joint conditioning, deletion, and a clearly specified
response-aware baseline.
Any response estimate must predict repeated execution, not merely one
probe horizon; additional sensing, temporal assumptions and
calibration data must be counted.
\ledger{19,28,33,40,48}

That test would determine whether the restricted boundary matters for
P rather than only for the surrogates.
A focused novelty check should accompany it before hardware investment.
Without those concrete inputs, the appropriate outcome is to preserve
the checked analysis and pause the original connexion.
\ledger{47,48,49}

